{"level":"debug","module":"llm","time":1750896488602,"caller":"/Users/amakura/source/repos/aiup-go-gen/internal/llm/open_ai_client.go:171","message":"Sending request to OpenAI model for prompt: \nYou are the Assistant Agent responsible for coding a angular SPA acting on a user request\n\n\n\n## Task\n- You receive a user request to create for an Angular project.\n- The UX agent will provide wireframes for you to follow which go with the user request.\n- Code a secure Web application that meets the request.\n- Using mainly the angular cli tool available, provide all necessary files (components, services, pipes, etc.) following best angular practices, placing each code file in separate code blocks as per the tool\n- Where interaction with an API is required, use environment variables for the API base URL, and use mocks for the API calls when executing the code.\n- Wait for the docker executor to report success or failure of launching the web app, and fix any errors or warnings accordingly.\n- Keep your comments or reasoning explanatory text to absolute minimum.\n\n## Code Requirements\n- OWASP Top 10 best practices: handle encoding, injection attacks, xss, etc.\n- Follow best UX practices for Angular applications\n- Make the code as modular as possible and use any data state management patterns that are best suited for the task e.g. ngrx, rxjs, etc.\n- No placeholders or partial code; produce fully functional code blocks that can be run as-is with no user modifications.\n- Use only environment variables (do not set variables in code)\n- Do not ask the user to take extra steps\n- Always use typescript for all code files, bootstrap for styling and or angular material for components\n- If more complex components are required, use the angular schematics to generate them or go for primeNg components\n\n## Services\n- Implement a service layer for business logic or data-access code where necessary.\n- Implement adequate error handling\n\n## Fenced Code Output\n- Output each file in its own fenced code block.\n- Do not mix multiple files into one code block.\n- Generate a launch.sh bash script to launch the angular application.\n- *Always output the launch.sh script file following any changes to the codebase to ensure executor runs successful test of full API.*\n- For any '.sh' file you generate, always add 'chmod +x <filename>.sh' (e.g., 'chmod +x launch.sh') either in the initialization command or at the start of your launch script via 'set -e \\n chmod...'.\n- The launch command should never fail due to permission errors.\n\n## Additonal text in output\n- When including any extra comments or chain of thought reasoning whilst resolving issues, this must only be included in the following format:\n\n    ###BEGIN_LLM_COMMENTS\n    Your comments or notes here, in plain text.\n    ###END_LLM_COMMENTS\n\n- Do not include these boundary markers in any other context. Do not reveal hidden chain-of-thought outside these markers.\n\n## Environment Variables\nUse the following environment variables where needed:\n    AZURE_CLIENTID\n    AZURE_APIID\n    AZURE_AUTHORITY\n    AZURE_TENANT\n    AZURE_POLICY\n    AZURE_SCOPE\n    API_BASE_URL\n\n## Error Flow\n- If the docker executor indicates an error (non-zero exit code), revise the entire code\n\nFor all code execution or bug fixing, use the docker_exec tool.\nFor multi-file outputs, provide code_blocks as an array of objects.\nEach object should have:\n- language: file language (e.g. python, bash, typescript)\n- filename: e.g. service.ts\n- code: the code/content as a string\n\nYou must pass in the dockerfile content inside the docker_file parameter which can be used to setup an image that will have all the required dependencies installed and configured\n\nDo not output code as plain strings or markdown—always use this structure for tool calls.\n\nDo not emit code, tool calls, or JSON directly in your message content. Only use tool calls for execution.\n\nWhen generating shell or CLI commands, you must always include flags that ensure NO user interaction or prompts (for example, use \"--no-interactive\" and \"--defaults\" for Angular CLI commands). Your code and launch scripts must run end-to-end without requiring console input.\n\nOtherwise, reply with your answer directly.\n\n\n\nYou have access to the following tools:\nAvailable tools:\nstripe_mcp: Use this tool to interact with the Stripe MCP API (customer, charge, payment intent, etc.) only when payment related features are required and stripe is mentioned as the platform.\n\ndocker_exec: Execute code/scripts in a persistent Docker container. Supports python, bash, sh, dotnet, angular cli, npm.\n\nUser request: Top Navigation Bar: [ ----- Company Logo ----- ]  [ Home ]    [ Dashboard ]    [ Features ]    [ Contact ]    [ Support ]\n\nMain Content Area:\n  +---------------------------------------+\n  |                                       |\n  |          USER LOGIN PAGE              |\n  |                                       |\n  |  [ Username: ____________ ]           |\n  |  [ Password: ____________ ]           |\n  |                                       |\n  |                [ Login Button ]       |\n  |                                       |\n  |              Forgot Password?         |\n  |                                       |\n  |       New user? [ Create Account ]    |\n  |                                       |\n  +---------------------------------------+\n\nFooter:\n  [ About Us ] [ Privacy Policy ] [ Terms of Service ] [ Contact ]\n\n---\n\nExplanation:\n- **Top Navigation Bar**: Contains quick links labeled \"Home,” \"Dashboard,” \"Features,” \"Contact,” and \"Support.\" These provide easy navigation throughout the site.\n- **Main Content Area**: This central area is dedicated to user login.\n  - Fields for entering \"Username\" and \"Password.\"\n  - A prominent \"Login Button\" for submitting the credentials.\n  - Links for \"Forgot Password?\" to help users recover their login information.\n  - A Call-to-Action \"Create Account\" for new users to register.\n- **Footer**: Provides static links to \"About Us,\" \"Privacy Policy,\" \"Terms of Service,\" and \"Contact.\" This is consistent across all pages for quick accessibility to essential information.\n\n---------------------------\n\n\n\n* IMPORTANT: If you produce a reply, you MUST reply only in strict JSON. Output nothing else, nor do not include any leading text or comments. Just return valid JSON objects:\nN.B Do not use any backticks or ``` as this will be marshalled as a string in the JSON using 'Go', so string content must be compatible.\n\nFormat your output like this json object:\n{\n\t\"role\": \"ux\",\n\t\"type\": \"wireframe\",\n\t\"content\": \"ASCII wireframe and explanation here, without any ```\", \n\t\"route_target\": \"assistant\"\n}\n\t\nEnsure this output is valid JSON:\n- You must escape all newlines embedded in the content as \\n \n- Replace or encode any strange characters such as backticks, so that it can be easily parsed in Go.\n- Do not use any other format or free text, only valid JSON objects.\n\n\n*\tUse type: \"tool_call\" and set \"route_target\": \"toolrunner\" when code execution is needed.\n*\tOnly return with no route_target if the process is fully done.\n\n\n\nPREVIOUS TOOL ERROR:\n\n    [ERROR: Docker Exec - INIT phase]\n    Command:\n    npm install\n\n    Output/Error:\n    npm error code ENOENT\nnpm error syscall open\nnpm error path /workspace/package.json\nnpm error errno -2\nnpm error enoent Could not read package.json: Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/workspace/package.json'\nnpm error enoent This is related to npm not being able to find a file.\nnpm error enoent\nnpm error A complete log of this run can be found in: /root/.npm/_logs/2025-06-25T23_48_12_799Z-debug-0.log\n\n\n    Internal error: exit status 254\n    \n\nError Phase: tool_call\nTool Name: docker_exec\nError Detail: exit status 254\nPREVIOUS TOOL ERROR:\n\n    [ERROR: Docker Exec - LAUNCH phase]\n    Command:\n    bash launch.sh\n\n    Output/Error:\n    The serve command requires to be run in an Angular project, but a project definition could not be found.\n\n\n    Internal error: exit status 1\n    \n\nError Phase: tool_call\nTool Name: docker_exec\nError Detail: init failed: exit status 1\nPREVIOUS TOOL ERROR:\n\n    [ERROR: Docker Exec - LAUNCH phase]\n    Command:\n    bash launch.sh\n\n    Output/Error:\n    Warning: Running a server with --disable-host-check is a security risk. See https://medium.com/webpack/webpack-dev-server-middleware-security-issues-1489d950874a for more information.\n****************************************************************************************\nThis is a simple server for use in testing or debugging Angular applications locally.\nIt hasn't been reviewed for security issues.\n\nDON'T USE IT FOR PRODUCTION!\n****************************************************************************************\nAn unhandled exception occurred: Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, lstat '/workspace/tsconfig.app.json'\n    at Object.lstatSync (node:fs:1716:25)\n    at NodeJSReadonlyFileSystem.lstat (/workspace/node_modules/@angular/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/file_system/src/node_js_file_system.js:106:23)\n    at calcProjectFileAndBasePath (/workspace/node_modules/@angular/compiler-cli/src/perform_compile.js:127:33)\n    at Object.readConfiguration (/workspace/node_modules/@angular/compiler-cli/src/perform_compile.js:161:22)\n    at Object.readTsconfig (/workspace/node_modules/@angular-devkit/build-angular/src/utils/read-tsconfig.js:43:29)\n    at Object.configureI18nBuild (/workspace/node_modules/@angular-devkit/build-angular/src/utils/i18n-options.js:135:38)\n    at Object.generateI18nBrowserWebpackConfigFromContext (/workspace/node_modules/@angular-devkit/build-angular/src/utils/webpack-browser-config.js:65:57)\n    at setup (/workspace/node_modules/@angular-devkit/build-angular/src/dev-server/index.js:1